1008:
(a) No person shall stop, stand or park a vehicle except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic or in compliance with law or the direction of a police officer or traffic control device in any of the following places:
(1) Between the front building restriction line and the front street line except on a bona fide paved driveway leading to a garage, carport, or other paved parking place;
(2) On a sidewalk or on the verge between the sidewalk and roadway;
(3) On the roadway side of any vehicle stopped or parked at the edge of a curb of a street;
(4) Within an intersection;
(5) On a crosswalk;
(6) Between a safety zone and the adjacent curb or within thirty (30) feet of points on the curb immediately opposite the ends of a safety zone, unless a different length is indicated by signs or markings;
(7) Alongside or opposite any street excavation or obstruction when stopping, standing, or parking would obstruct traffic;
(8) On any railroad tracks;
(9) Upon any bridge or other elevated structure upon a highway;
(10) At any curb marked in yellow by direction of the city manager and chief of police; or
(11) At any place prohibited by the city manager and chief of police or by ordinance and indicated by official signs or markings.
(b) No person shall stand or park a vehicle, whether occupied or not, except momentarily to pick up or discharge a passenger in any of the following places:
(1) In front of or within three (3) feet of a public or private driveway or in such a manner as to block ingress or egress thereof;
(2) Within fifteen (15) feet of a fire hydrant;
(3) Within twenty (20) feet of a crosswalk at an intersection;
(4) Within thirty (30) feet upon the approach to any flashing signal, stop sign, yield sign, or traffic-control signal located at the side of the roadway;
(5) Within twenty (20) feet of the driveway entrance to any fire station or, on the side of a street opposite the entrance to any fire station, within seventy-five (75) feet of such entrance when so marked; or
(6) Within any area marked as a bus stop.
(c) No person shall park a vehicle, whether occupied or not, except temporarily for the purpose of and while actually engaged in loading or unloading property or passengers within fifty (50) feet of the nearest rail of a railroad crossing.
(d) No person shall move a vehicle not lawfully under such person's control either into any prohibited area or move and leave such vehicle away from a curb such distance as is prohibited by this chapter or other ordinance.

1038:
It shall be unlawful for any person to operate an off-road vehicle off the roadway or on the property of another under the following circumstances:
(1) Without operative brakes, or without mufflers or other silencing equipment;
(2) On the private property of another without first obtaining permission from the landowner, lessee or other person in control of the property; permission shall not be required where notice is conspicuously posted on the property declaring it open to off-road vehicles;
(3) Within one thousand (1,000) feet of any residence, place of worship, business or public meeting place between 9:00 p.m. and 9:00 a.m. except where the off-road vehicle operator is the owner, lessee or invited guest of the owner or lessee of the residence, place of worship, business or public meeting place.

1081:
(a) The city manager, or his authorized representative, shall have the power to administer and enforce all regulations and procedures adopted to implement this article, including the right to maintain an action or proceeding in any court of competent jurisdiction to compel compliance with or restrain any violation of this article, or to seek recovery of damages, fees or costs.
(b) Subject to approval by the city commission, the city manager can:
(1) Administer, coordinate and oversee conservation, acquisition, extension, improvement, design, construction, management, operation and maintenance of the city municipal separate storm sewer system;
(2) Establish or oversee establishment of regulations for control, management, and enhancement of the quality or quantity of stormwater, and establish procedures and guidelines to implement such regulations;
(3) Determine the manner in which stormwater conveyances should be operated;
(4) Inspect and enforce private stormwater conveyances which discharge to the city municipal separate storm sewer system;
(5) Advise the city commission and other city departments on issues related to stormwater;
(6) Protect the city municipal separate storm sewer system and stormwater conveyances and properties owned or maintained by the city and prescribe how they are used by others;
(7) Require new, increased, or significantly changed stormwater discharges to the city municipal separate storm sewer system to comply with the terms of this article, including regulations and procedures adopted pursuant thereto, with respect to the installation of stormwater conveyances or best management practices;
(8) Develop programs or procedures to control the discharge of pollutants into the city municipal separate storm sewer system;
(9) Develop, adopt and implement the city stormwater management program; and
(10) Comply with federal and state regulations and permits concerning the city municipal separate storm sewer system.
(Ord. No. 0415, § 1, 12-21-04)

1087:
(a) The following categories of discharges and connections are exempt from the prohibitions set forth in sections 20-8 and 20-9, unless the city manager determines such a discharge or connection to be a significant source of pollution.
(1) Water line flushing performed or approved by the engineering department or the county water and sewer department and other unpolluted discharges from potable water sources;
(2) Unpolluted landscape irrigation and lawn watering;
(3) Unpolluted irrigation water;
(4) Unpolluted diverted stream flows;
(5) Unpolluted rising or pumped ground water;
(6) Unpolluted infiltration to the city municipal separate storm sewer system;
(7) Unpolluted water from foundation and footing drains, crawl space pumps, and air conditioning condensation;
(8) Unpolluted springs;
(9) Individual residential car washing where biodegradable soap is used;
(10) Unpolluted flows from riparian habitats and wetlands;
(11) Unpolluted dechlorinated swimming pool discharges;
(12) Flows from firefighting; or
(13) Other unpolluted water.
(b) In the event that the city manager determines a discharge or connection to constitute a significant source of pollution, then the city manager shall notify the discharger of such determination, that such discharge or connection is now prohibited by the terms of this article, and that the discharger shall be required to immediately cease the illicit discharge or illicit connection and to comply with the provisions of this article.
(c) The discharger, after notice, shall be required to immediately cease the illicit discharge or illicit connection and to bring any discharge or connection to the city municipal separate storm sewer system into compliance with the provisions of this article.
(Ord. No. 0415, § 1, 12-21-04)

1094:
Whenever required to carry out the objectives of this article, including but not limited to obtaining information regarding permit compliance, implementing the city stormwater management program, or determining whether any person is in violation of any provision of this article, the city manager may in writing require a discharger to the city municipal separate storm sewer system to:
(1) Establish and maintain records;
(2) Make reports;
(3) Install, use and maintain monitoring equipment or methods, including where appropriate, biological monitoring methods;
(4) Sample such discharges, in accordance with such methods, at such locations, at such intervals, and in such manner as the city manager shall prescribe; and
(5) Provide such other information as he or she may reasonably require.
(Ord. No. 0415, § 1, 12-21-04)
